{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1786210148,"nonce":"UfsANSnhkpQkE0Zon8KIBe0D5fc=","target":"0ac132b522debc3e03e52d3cba81ed468264cb346c8229f853868201adb62ba1","message":"**What is wrong.** The group rollup is recomputed once per opened occurrence.\n`alertObserver.joinMembers` (`internal/app/adapters.go:909-919`) loops over the\nbatch's outcomes calling `grouping.Join` for each, and `Join`\n(`internal/grouping/service/service.go:291`) ends with\n`s.recompute(ctx, scope, groupID, at)` — which does a full `members.Rollup`\naggregate over the group plus a `SetRollup` CAS write\n(`service.go:397-420`), and then `evaluateStorm`. A 500-alert Alertmanager batch\nthat opens 500 occurrences in one group therefore performs 500 full rollups and\n500 versioned writes on the same row, serialised by the CAS.\n\n**Why it matters.** A storm is precisely when this happens, and storm handling is\na core requirement — the damping in ADR 0020 and the storm latch exist because\n500-alert batches are the expected bad day. Contention on one `alert_groups` row\nat O(n) makes ingestion slowest exactly when Alertmanager is retrying hardest, and\n`internal/ingestion/api/shed.go:68` notes the retry budget is only about five\nminutes before Alertmanager gives up and the alerts are lost silently (ADR 0007).\nThe rollup is also idempotent within a batch: 499 of the 500 results are\ndiscarded.\n\n**Where.** `internal/app/adapters.go:900-928`,\n`internal/grouping/service/service.go:270-300, 384-430`,\n`internal/grouping/service/ports.go:59` (`Rollup`).\n\n**Done when.** One batch produces one rollup per affected group regardless of how\nmany members it opened (join the members, then recompute once), storm evaluation\nruns once per group per batch, and a benchmark or integration test over a\n500-alert single-group batch shows the rollup count is O(groups) not O(alerts) and\nrecords the wall-clock before and after.","files":null}]}